  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origins
    
    The Dawn Mandate was enacted by the Council of Luminaries, Aurora Stack's governing body, during the early phases of the Orca Incursion. Its immediate catalyst was the emergence of ☽'Iarlocias the Silent — a figure of disputed status who surfaced from the deep bearing orca-blood and speaking prophecies the aurora would not confirm. The crystal spires at The Still Pools, a geothermally-heated dissident settlement at the Berg's edge, had already begun fracturing. Rather than risk a governance vote under what the Luminaries termed "clouded skies," they imposed a requirement: only citizens capable of publicly manifesting aurora-light before witnesses retained voice in governance.
    
    The Mandate replaced the older Stillness-Bound accords, though no surviving copy of those accords has been recovered from ice-memory. It was declared temporary.
    
    ## Persistence and Scope
    
    Three succession crises have since passed under the Mandate's authority — Aurora-touched (crown-contested), Of The First Shelf (ice-wept), and the economic fracture of Collapse (ice-cracked) — each deepening its precedent. The subsumption of the Dawn-Marked classification into its framework formalized the status of disenfranchised citizens as a legal category rather than a temporary condition.
    
    Enforcement rests with the Beacon-Keepers, who observe public galleries during votes and record whose flippers remain dark. A certification path theoretically exists: a citizen marked dark may petition a registered aurora-adept to witness a manifestation. In practice, willing adepts are scarce. A notice board in Second Spire Landing recently carried a plea for exactly such certification — one posting among many, unremarkable in its desperation.
    
    ## Assessment
    
    The Nightshelf considers the Mandate proof that Aurora Stack governance selects for compliance, not capacity. The ice-memory in the relevant Foundation stratum records the Still Pools collapse plainly enough. What it does not record is anyone voting to make the emergency permanent. That, apparently, required no vote at all.
